WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
Graph questions look easy, which is why they're

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.200
easy to lose marks on.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.280
Across the last twelve Chemistry and Biology marking

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.040
feedback reports, eleven commented on graphs drawn or

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.560
read poorly, and nine on trends described without

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.280
the numbers.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.320
So here's how to read a graph, draw

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.880
one that earns its marks, describe a trend

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.280
with data, and know when you can extend

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.000
the line.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.720
Before you read any value, read the axes:

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.840
what's measured, in what units, and what one

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.440
small square is worth.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.480
Then go from the axis to the line,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.720
across to the other axis, and write the

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.320
value with its unit.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.600
If the line falls between gridlines, estimate to

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.280
about half a small square.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.880
A number without its unit can cost the

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:48.480
mark.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.660
Here's an HSC-style Biology question.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.980
A hospital laboratory tested E.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.740
coli samples for resistance to antibiotic K, and

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.460
the table shows the percentage resistant in five

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.580
years from twenty ten to twenty twenty.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.660
There are four parts: plot it, describe the

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.620
trend, estimate one value, and assess a prediction.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.740
If you take Chemistry, the skills are identical.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.780
Pause here to answer all four parts.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:28.920
Part a.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.520
Put the independent variable, the year, along the

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.000
bottom, and the percentage up the side.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.080
Each axis needs even steps, every gridline worth

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:39.720
the same.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.080
Here's the trap: the years jump by two,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.320
then by three.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.240
Space the five years evenly, and you squash

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.600
the later gaps and bend the trend.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.280
Place each year at its real value, and

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.080
choose a scale that fills most of the

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.600
grid, because a bigger plot lets you read

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.200
values more precisely.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.960
Label each axis with what's measured and its

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.000
unit, and add a title.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.440
Plot each point as a small, clear cross.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.440
Then draw a line of best fit: one

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.000
smooth line that follows the trend, with the

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.800
points scattered about evenly on both sides, as

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:16.720
they are here.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.280
Don't join the dots.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.240
Real data has scatter, and dot to dot

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.320
treats every wobble as real.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.360
Title, axes, intervals, labels, scale: TAILS.

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:32.166
Part b: describe the trend.

00:02:32.447 --> 00:02:35.246
Say the direction, then prove it with data.

00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:38.286
Resistance rose steadily, from four per cent in

00:02:38.407 --> 00:02:40.766
twenty ten to nineteen per cent in twenty

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.406
twenty, about one and a half percentage points

00:02:43.487 --> 00:02:44.326
a year.

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:47.686
If the pattern changes somewhere, say where.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.686
Here it doesn't, so steadily is the right

00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:51.366
word.

00:02:51.647 --> 00:02:53.886
Direction, data, change.

00:02:54.527 --> 00:02:57.006
And if the question says explain, give a

00:02:57.087 --> 00:03:01.406
plausible scientific reason: antibiotic use can select resistant

00:03:01.487 --> 00:03:03.726
bacteria, so they could make up more of

00:03:03.807 --> 00:03:04.526
the samples.

00:03:04.927 --> 00:03:07.246
The graph alone doesn't show that cause.

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:10.226
One trap with percentages.

00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:13.986
Resistance rose from four to nineteen per cent.

00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:17.026
That's a rise of fifteen percentage points, the

00:03:17.107 --> 00:03:18.546
gap between the two numbers.

00:03:19.107 --> 00:03:22.146
But compared with where it started, nineteen is

00:03:22.347 --> 00:03:24.946
almost five times four, a rise of about

00:03:25.107 --> 00:03:27.186
three hundred and seventy five per cent.

00:03:27.827 --> 00:03:30.066
Both are true, so say which one you

00:03:30.227 --> 00:03:30.626
mean.

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:34.953
Part c asks for twenty sixteen, which sits

00:03:35.034 --> 00:03:36.473
inside the data.

00:03:36.594 --> 00:03:40.433
That's interpolation: reading between measured points.

00:03:40.714 --> 00:03:42.793
Go up from twenty sixteen to your line

00:03:42.874 --> 00:03:45.673
of best fit, not to the nearest point,

00:03:45.754 --> 00:03:48.633
then across to the axis: about thirteen per

00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:49.433
cent.

00:03:49.554 --> 00:03:51.513
Draw your read-off lines on the graph, so

00:03:51.554 --> 00:03:53.193
a marker can follow them.

00:03:53.354 --> 00:03:55.913
Reading inside the data is fairly safe, because

00:03:55.994 --> 00:03:58.233
measured points on both sides hold the line

00:03:58.314 --> 00:03:59.113
in place.

00:04:00.334 --> 00:04:01.933
Part d is different.

00:04:02.494 --> 00:04:05.293
Twenty forty is twenty years past the last

00:04:05.374 --> 00:04:05.773
point.

00:04:06.174 --> 00:04:10.013
That's extrapolation, extending the line beyond the data.

00:04:10.574 --> 00:04:12.733
The line does reach about fifty per cent.

00:04:13.294 --> 00:04:15.693
But that assumes resistance keeps rising at the

00:04:15.774 --> 00:04:18.733
same rate for twenty years, and new drugs,

00:04:18.854 --> 00:04:21.453
prescribing or infection control could change it.

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:23.853
So the further the line goes past the

00:04:23.934 --> 00:04:27.053
data, the weaker the value, and this prediction

00:04:27.134 --> 00:04:28.093
is weak.

00:04:28.494 --> 00:04:30.653
Inside the data, read it.

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:32.573
Outside, doubt it.

00:04:32.614 --> 00:04:34.413
And if a question asks you to extend

00:04:34.454 --> 00:04:37.293
a line, do it, and state the assumption.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.060
One more drawing choice.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:43.060
Use a line graph for a continuous relationship

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.620
or a change over time, like these years

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:46.900
and percentages.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.220
If the independent variable is a set of

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:53.460
categories, like different antibiotics, use a column graph,

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.100
with gaps between the columns, because there's nothing

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.780
in between two antibiotics.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.140
In one recent Chemistry guideline, a column graph

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.260
earned full marks only with an accurate scale,

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.980
axis labels and correct column heights.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:08.760
Your turn.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.600
Do the one in your subject, or both.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.040
Chemistry first.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.360
A student collected carbon dioxide from marble chips

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.960
and acid, and drew the graph on screen.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.200
Identify three problems with her graph, and explain

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.680
how to correct each.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.240
Pause here to answer.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:38.866
Her time axis gives thirty, sixty, a hundred

00:05:38.947 --> 00:05:41.266
and twenty and two hundred and forty seconds

00:05:41.427 --> 00:05:44.946
equal gaps, so the scale isn't even: use

00:05:45.107 --> 00:05:46.266
equal steps.

00:05:46.547 --> 00:05:49.346
Her volume axis has no unit: label it

00:05:49.507 --> 00:05:52.146
volume of carbon dioxide, in millilitres.

00:05:52.547 --> 00:05:55.586
And she joined the dots: draw one smooth

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:57.106
curve of best fit.

00:05:57.347 --> 00:05:59.586
The habit to drop: a line of best

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:01.106
fit must be straight.

00:06:01.507 --> 00:06:03.826
When the data curve, it's a curve.

00:06:05.034 --> 00:06:06.353
Now Biology.

00:06:06.634 --> 00:06:09.033
A student measured the zone of inhibition around

00:06:09.114 --> 00:06:13.113
discs soaked in an antiseptic at five concentrations.

00:06:13.194 --> 00:06:15.433
Her mean results are in the table.

00:06:15.674 --> 00:06:19.353
Describe the relationship between antiseptic concentration and zone

00:06:19.434 --> 00:06:20.473
diameter.

00:06:20.634 --> 00:06:21.833
Pause here to answer.

00:06:32.234 --> 00:06:34.473
From zero to three per cent, the zone

00:06:34.554 --> 00:06:38.313
grew steadily, from six to fifteen millimetres.

00:06:38.394 --> 00:06:40.393
From three to six per cent, it grew

00:06:40.474 --> 00:06:43.673
only one more millimetre, so it levelled off.

00:06:43.754 --> 00:06:46.473
That's direction, data and change.

00:06:46.554 --> 00:06:50.073
The answer to drop: as concentration increases, the

00:06:50.154 --> 00:06:51.513
zone increases.

00:06:51.594 --> 00:06:53.593
It has no numbers, and it misses the

00:06:53.674 --> 00:06:54.473
levelling off.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.380
So, for a graph question.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.260
Read the axes and units first.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.300
Draw with TAILS, even steps, and a line

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:05.700
or curve of best fit, not dot to

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:06.340
dot.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.180
Describe with direction, data and any change, and

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.580
say whether you mean percentage points or per

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:13.060
cent.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:15.540
Inside the data, read it.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.740
Outside, doubt it, and state your assumption.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:23.940
Next: practical questions, and fixes that actually fix.

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:26.606
Here are the key notes.

00:07:26.847 --> 00:07:28.766
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:28.847 --> 00:07:29.646
to copy them.
